<<declare $npc_relationship = 50>>
<<declare $npc_can_talk = true>>
<<declare $npc_can_trade = true>>
<<declare $npc_buy_price_multiplier = 1.0>>

<<declare $gh_met_harbourmaster = false>>
<<declare $gh_heard_missing_boat_warning = false>>
<<declare $gh_accepted_sounding_job = false>>
<<declare $gh_asked_about_bell = false>>
<<declare $gh_harbourmaster_trust = 0>>
<<declare $gh_met_mara = false>>
<<declare $gh_mara_trust = 0>>
<<declare $gh_met_tomas = false>>
<<declare $gh_tomas_trust = 0>>
<<declare $gh_met_inez = false>>
<<declare $gh_inez_trust = 0>>
<<declare $gh_clue_bell_log = false>>
<<declare $gh_clue_cold_catch = false>>
<<declare $gh_clue_hull_marks = false>>
<<declare $gh_clue_net_return = false>>

title: Greyhaven_Harbourmaster
---
<<if $npc_relationship < 15>>
Harbourmaster: No. I have heard enough about the way you conduct yourself. The office is open to the harbour, not to you.
-> Leave.
<<else>>
<<if not $gh_met_harbourmaster>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Harbourmaster: You're new to this quay. Keep your distance from the office until I know what sort of hand you are.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: New boat, unfamiliar face. Learn the channel marks before you try the outer water.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Harbourmaster: You're the new hand on the old boat, then. Keep her inside the buoys until you know how the channel behaves.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: Heard you were working these waters. Good. A harbour is safer when its crews know one another.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Harbourmaster: I've heard a little about you already. Let's see whether the reports match the person.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: There you are. Someone I can put a little faith in, by the sound of it. Welcome to Greyhaven.
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Harbourmaster: Back again. I'll answer what I must. Don't mistake that for trust.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: You're persistent. Keep your boat clear of the working channel and we won't have a problem.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Harbourmaster: Back already? The channel has not become any kinder.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: Back again. I was about to ask whether you had found anything worth reporting.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Harbourmaster: There you are. I kept a space in the log for your return.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: There you are. I was beginning to wonder when you'd bring that boat back in. The quay is better for seeing a familiar hull.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

-> Ask about the missing boats.
    <<if not $gh_heard_missing_boat_warning>>
        <<set $gh_heard_missing_boat_warning = true>>
        Harbourmaster: Three boats went out past the outer marker. Only one came back.
        Harbourmaster: Its skipper would not speak about what followed him.
        Harbourmaster: He kept asking whether the bell was still ringing.
        <<relationship "harbourmaster.listened_to_missing_boat_warning" 2>>
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: The same three boats. One returned. I won't dress an unanswered count up as a story.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: The same three boats. The same one that returned. Nobody has found the other two.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: Three went out. One returned. I still check the water when a late boat comes through, even when I know it cannot be them.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about the lighthouse bell.
    <<if not $gh_asked_about_bell>>
        <<set $gh_asked_about_bell = true>>
        Harbourmaster: The lighthouse bell? It has been silent for eleven years.
        Harbourmaster: Do not answer a bell you cannot place.
        <<set $gh_harbourmaster_trust = $gh_harbourmaster_trust + 1>>
        <<relationship "harbourmaster.asked_about_silent_bell" 2>>
        <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
            Harbourmaster: I have checked the tower often enough to know the difference between a bell and a sound carried over water.
        <<endif>>
    <<else>>
        <<if $npc_relationship < 60>>
            Harbourmaster: Eleven years. It has not rung. Leave it there.
        <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
            Harbourmaster: Still silent. I would rather you trusted the marks on the water than a sound in the dark.
        <<else>>
            Harbourmaster: Eleven years of silence. If you hear it, don't come looking for me first. Get your boat inside the markers.
        <<endif>>
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what a normal harbour day looks like.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: Count boats in. Count boats out. Keep the channel clear. It is not complicated.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: Check the markers, log departures, settle quay disputes, and make sure the last crew has somewhere to tie up.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: I start before the market opens. First I check the marker lights, then the tide notes, then the names that have not come back. The last part takes longer than it should.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about the channel markers.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: Red and green mark the working channel. Read the local chart. Don't guess from memory.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: Red and green mark the channel edges. They are warnings, not decorations. Give the piers room when the tide turns.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: The markers tell you where the safe water should be. The word should matters. Watch the water as closely as the lights.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what he watches when the weather changes.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Harbourmaster: The waterline and the wind. That's enough for now.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Harbourmaster: Wind at the headland, chop at the harbour mouth, and whether the boats are turning home earlier than usual.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: I watch the headland first. Then the channel mouth. A change in the wind reaches the smaller boats before it reaches the office.
        Harbourmaster: If the birds leave the water all at once, I stop treating the afternoon as ordinary.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask for one of his harbour sayings.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Harbourmaster: "A safe return is worth more than a fast departure." Remember that.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Harbourmaster: "A harbour is a promise, not a fence." We keep it by looking after the people who use it.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: My old supervisor used to say, "Count the boats you see, and leave room in the book for the ones you don't." I used to think it was poor bookkeeping.
    <<endif>>

-> Promise to respect the channel marks.
    Harbourmaster: Good. I can work with a promise that keeps your hull off someone else's pier.
    <<relationship "harbourmaster.promised_channel_discipline" 2>>

-> Tell him the warnings are superstition.
    Harbourmaster: Call it what you like. I have buried people who thought the sea was bluffing.
    <<relationship "harbourmaster.dismissed_harbour_warning" -3>>

-> Ask for work.
    <<if not $gh_accepted_sounding_job>>
        Harbourmaster: A sounding buoy washed in near the western mudflat.
        Harbourmaster: Bring it back intact. Do not switch it on.
        Harbourmaster: If it starts answering, drop the line.
        <<set $gh_accepted_sounding_job = true>>
        <<relationship "harbourmaster.accepted_sounding_buoy_job" 3>>
        <<checkpoint "greyhaven.sounding_buoy_accepted">>
    <<elseif $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Harbourmaster: The western mudflat. Bring the buoy back intact. I trust you remember why I asked.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: The western mudflat. Bring the buoy back intact. And keep it switched off.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what he does after the last boat returns. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Harbourmaster: I finish the log, check the moorings once more, and wait until the harbour sounds like a harbour again.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Harbourmaster: Quiet is useful. Silence is different. I know the difference now.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what he has written in the log about you. <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
    Harbourmaster: That you came back when you said you would. That you listened when I warned you. I don't write praise often, but I write what I see.
    Harbourmaster: Keep giving me reasons to leave that entry unchanged.

-> Ask what the lighthouse log says about the bell.
    <<if not $gh_clue_bell_log>>
        Harbourmaster: The last confirmed ringing is eleven years back. After that, the book records inspections, not a single sounding.
        Harbourmaster: No entry explains the survivor's question. The log can tell you when the bell stopped. It cannot tell you what he heard.
        <<set $gh_clue_bell_log = true>>
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: Eleven years since the last confirmed ring. The inspections continue; the soundings do not.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask how Greyhaven's old channel charts changed. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Harbourmaster: The older charts disagree around the outer shoal. Sand shifts, storms move markers, and a line that was safe for one season can be a poor guide the next.
    Harbourmaster: That's why I trust a chart as a record, not a promise. You still have to read the water.
<<endif>>


-> Compare the records from the quay. <<if $gh_clue_bell_log or $gh_clue_cold_catch or $gh_clue_hull_marks or $gh_clue_net_return>>
    <<if $gh_clue_bell_log and $gh_clue_cold_catch and $gh_clue_hull_marks and $gh_clue_net_return>>
        Harbourmaster: Mara's market notes, Tomas's hull measurements, and Inez's preserved line are not the same kind of evidence. That's precisely why I take them seriously.
    <<else>>
        Harbourmaster: We have fragments, not a pattern. A log, a catch, a mark or a piece of line can tell us what happened to that one thing. It cannot explain the whole sea.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>
<<set $gh_met_harbourmaster = true>>
<<endif>>
===

title: Greyhaven_Fishmonger
---
<<if $npc_relationship < 15>>
Mara Venn: Not today. I won't weigh a thing for someone I cannot trust.
-> Leave.
<<else>>
<<if not $gh_met_mara>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Mara Venn: First time at my stall? Keep the catch where I can see it and don't touch the scales.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: I'm Mara. Put your catch down gently. I can tell a rough landing by the bruises.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Mara Venn: First time at my stall? Mara Venn. I keep the market open while there's something worth weighing.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: I've seen your boat around. I'm Mara. If you handle your catch carefully, we'll get along.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Mara Venn: There you are. I've heard you bring your catch in clean. Let's see what the water gave you.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: Good to see you. I kept the better scale free. You have earned that much.
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Mara Venn: I remember you. Keep your hands off the scales until we've settled a few things.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: Back again. Let me see the catch before you tell me how good it is.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Mara Venn: Back with another hold of surprises? Let me see what the water gave you.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: There you are. How was the water today? You look like you've got a story and a full hold.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Mara Venn: I was wondering when you'd come by. I saved a clean space on the table for your catch.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: There you are. The market feels a little less quiet when a familiar boat comes in.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

-> Ask which fish are common around Greyhaven.
    Mara Venn: Mullet, milkfish, sillago. They are ordinary fish, and I mean that as a compliment.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
        Mara Venn: When the water is right, I see more variety. When it isn't, the same few species keep the market going.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what makes a catch worth buying.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Mara Venn: Sound condition. Clean handling. Honest weight. Start there.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Mara Venn: I look at the skin, eyes, gills and firmness. Bruising tells me how the fish was handled before it reached my table.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Mara Venn: Condition first, then freshness, then size. A large fish in poor condition is still a poor catch.
        Mara Venn: And tell me if it came from strange water. I'd rather inspect the truth than price a lie.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: A good catch is one you can explain. Where you found it, how long it was aboard, and how you kept it. The story matters because the fish cannot tell me.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask how she keeps the market running.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: Ice, clean boards, fair weights. The simple things. People notice when you stop doing them.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: I sort the early deliveries, keep the ice covered, clean between batches and write down what sells. The market is mostly small chores done on time.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: I open before the first boats return. I check the ice, rinse the boards, set the scales, and leave one space empty for the people who are late.
        Mara Venn: Some mornings that space stays empty all day.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about keeping fish fresh on the boat.
    Mara Venn: Keep the catch shaded and cool. Don't leave it piled in the sun while you finish another job.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Mara Venn: Separate a damaged fish from the rest. One bruised catch should not ruin the quality of everything beside it.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about the cold fish.
    Mara Venn: Lately, a few catches arrive cold in the middle. Not spoiled. Just cold, as if they were lifted from deeper water.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: I don't know what it means, and I won't invent an answer.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: I've checked the ice and the storage. It doesn't explain every case.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: I started keeping notes. Same cold centre, different boats, different days. I have no explanation, but I know it isn't just one careless fisherman.
    <<endif>>

-> Tell Mara exactly what you found in the cold catch.
    Mara Venn: Good. Don't hide a strange catch from the person weighing it. I won't promise a good price, but I won't pretend I didn't see it.
    <<relationship "mara.was_honest_about_unusual_catch" 4>>

-> Insist the catch is ordinary and hide what you noticed.
    Mara Venn: I know the smell of a clean catch. Don't ask me to put my name on a lie.
    <<relationship "mara.misrepresented_unusual_catch" -5>>

-> Ask about the missing boats.
    Mara Venn: One of the missing crews owed me for ice. I still set aside their crate every morning.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Mara Venn: Habit is a stubborn thing. Hope is worse.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Mara Venn: I keep the crate in the same place. I know the difference between waiting and expecting.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: I still write their names beside the unpaid ice. Not for the money. So I don't let the market forget who used to stand here.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about doing business.
    <<if not $npc_can_trade>>
        Mara Venn: I won't buy from someone who hasn't given me a reason to trust them. Start with honesty.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: I'll consider sound, ordinary catch. Nothing questionable, and no favours.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Mara Venn: We can do business at the usual rate. Keep the catch clean and the weights honest.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: We've done all right by each other. I'll make sure you aren't treated like a stranger.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Mara Venn: I trust your handling now. I can offer a better rate when the catch deserves it.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: You've earned a place here. I can offer the best rate I give anyone in Greyhaven.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask for one of her market sayings.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Mara Venn: "A clean table is cheaper than a spoiled catch." Keep your work tidy.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Mara Venn: "Freshness is a promise the buyer cannot check for themselves." That's why I don't cut corners.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: My mother used to say, "Never let a good day's catch make you careless with tomorrow's." I understand it better now.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what she does after closing. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Mara Venn: I count the day's sales, clean the scales and put tomorrow's ice order together.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Mara Venn: Then I check the empty crate. I know it doesn't bring anyone back. I haven't found a reason to stop.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask to see the notes she keeps about strange catches. <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
    Mara Venn: Only the observations, not the names of the people who brought them in. I won't put a neighbour's fear on display.
    Mara Venn: Here. Dates, storage conditions, and where each catch was found. That's all I know for certain.

-> Ask why some catches arrive cold in the middle.
    <<if not $gh_clue_cold_catch>>
        Mara Venn: It isn't the ice. I've checked the storage and asked different crews how long the catch was aboard.
        Mara Venn: The cold sits inside the fish, not across the surface. It has happened on separate boats. I can record that much; I cannot tell you why.
        <<set $gh_clue_cold_catch = true>>
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: Different boats, same odd cold centre. I still have no honest explanation for it.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what she does with the market after a bad season. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Mara Venn: I buy less, waste less, and make sure the smaller crews aren't pushed out by one poor week. A market survives by keeping people able to return.
    Mara Venn: My mother taught me that an empty stall is not always a failed business. Sometimes it means someone is still out working.
<<endif>>


-> Compare the records from the quay. <<if $gh_clue_bell_log or $gh_clue_cold_catch or $gh_clue_hull_marks or $gh_clue_net_return>>
    <<if $gh_clue_bell_log and $gh_clue_cold_catch and $gh_clue_hull_marks and $gh_clue_net_return>>
        Mara Venn: The Harbourmaster has dates. Tomas has measurements. Inez has the actual line. I have the catch notes. I won't turn four observations into one story just because a story would be easier to sell.
    <<else>>
        Mara Venn: We have fragments, not a pattern. A log, a catch, a mark or a piece of line can tell us what happened to that one thing. It cannot explain the whole sea.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>
<<set $gh_met_mara = true>>
<<endif>>
===

title: Greyhaven_Mechanic
---
<<if $npc_relationship < 15>>
Tomas Vale: Take the boat elsewhere. I won't put my tools near it, and I won't discuss repairs with you.
-> Leave.
<<else>>
<<if not $gh_met_tomas>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Tomas Vale: Stop there. I don't know you or your engine. Tell me what's broken and keep your hands off my tools.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Tomas Vale: Tomas. I keep the repair shed running. Let me hear that engine idle before you tell me what's wrong.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Tomas Vale: Stop there. I want to hear that engine idle before you tell me what's wrong with it.
        Tomas Vale: Tomas Vale. Mostly engines, sometimes boats, occasionally bad decisions.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Tomas Vale: I've seen your boat around. Bring it close enough for me to hear the idle. I can usually tell a lot before the cover comes off.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Tomas Vale: There you are. Your engine has a familiar note now. Let's hear what it has to say today.
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: Good. A boat I know, and a skipper who listens. That's a better start than most repairs get.
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Tomas Vale: I remember you. Say what you came to say, then give me room to work.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Tomas Vale: What has changed since the last time? Be specific.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Tomas Vale: That engine still sounds like it has something to confess.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Tomas Vale: Back again. You are getting better at noticing trouble before it gets expensive.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Tomas Vale: I know that engine note now. You bring problems in before they become wrecks. I appreciate that.
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: I kept the bench clear. You know where to put the boat, and I know you'll tell me what happened.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

-> Ask what a healthy outboard should sound like.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Tomas Vale: Even idle. No sudden clatter, no new vibration. If the sound changes, stop pretending you didn't hear it.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Tomas Vale: Listen at idle first, then under light load. A change in pitch can tell you the engine is working harder than it should.
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: Learn its ordinary sound. Not the sound you think it should make—the sound it actually makes when it's healthy. That's your baseline.
        Tomas Vale: I can tell your engine is struggling before the gauge catches up.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what to check before leaving the harbour.
    Tomas Vale: Fuel, propeller, steering response and anything loose that can move under vibration.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
        Tomas Vale: On an outboard, look for the cooling-water telltale too. If it disappears, don't keep running the engine to see what happens.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about the knocking beneath boats.
    Tomas Vale: I've heard knocks travel through a hull. Loose fittings, trapped timber, a line caught under the keel. There are explanations.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Tomas Vale: The trouble starts when the knocking keeps pace with the engine.
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: The trouble starts when the knocking keeps pace with the engine. I've checked the obvious fittings twice. I don't like saying that because it leaves me with fewer answers.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about the boat that returned.
    Tomas Vale: The boat that came back had fresh scoring under the waterline. No matching scrape on the rocks. I logged it as unknown damage.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Tomas Vale: I checked the length and direction of the marks. They don't read like a single impact against stone.
    <<endif>>
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
        Tomas Vale: I kept the measurements. I won't call it proof of anything, but I won't throw away a record because it makes people uncomfortable.
    <<endif>>

-> Admit the engine has been making a worse noise than you first said.
    Tomas Vale: That's useful. I can work with an honest problem. I can't work with a story built to save someone's pride.
    <<relationship "tomas.admitted_engine_problem" 4>>

-> Dismiss his advice as needless caution.
    Tomas Vale: Fine. It's your engine. Don't bring me a preventable failure and call it bad luck.
    <<relationship "tomas.dismissed_repair_advice" -3>>

-> Ask why he marks repaired bolts with chalk. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Tomas Vale: A chalk line across a fastener and the fixed surface gives you a quick visual check. If the marks shift, something moved.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Tomas Vale: It isn't a substitute for a proper inspection. It's a reminder not to trust your memory over the evidence.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask who taught him to repair engines. <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
    Tomas Vale: Mostly mistakes. I broke things I could not afford to replace, then paid a better mechanic to show me what I had missed.
    Tomas Vale: I learned to write down every fix. Memory is not a tool.

-> Ask for one of his repair sayings.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Tomas Vale: "A small noise is cheaper than a large silence." Don't ignore it.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Tomas Vale: "An engine tells you its troubles twice: once in a whisper, once in a tow bill."
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: "Measure twice, tighten once, and never trust a machine you haven't listened to." I keep that one on the wall.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what repair he refuses to hurry. <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
    Tomas Vale: Fault tracing. If I cannot explain what failed, I won't tell someone the boat is fixed.
    Tomas Vale: A shortcut that sends a damaged boat back to sea isn't a repair. It's a delayed accident.

-> Leave.
    Tomas Vale: Don't ignore a change in engine pitch. The boat usually tells you before it quits.

-> Ask him to compare the returned hull's marks with a rock strike.
    <<if not $gh_clue_hull_marks>>
        Tomas Vale: The scoring runs along the hull. I found no matching stone transfer and no single impact point that explains the whole length.
        Tomas Vale: That rules out one easy explanation. It does not tell me what made the marks.
        <<set $gh_clue_hull_marks = true>>
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: Long scoring, no matching transfer, no clean impact point. Still an unknown cause.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask why he keeps old radio parts on the workbench. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Tomas Vale: I repair receivers when the engines are quiet. Mostly weather bands and harbour traffic. I like knowing which part of a signal is equipment and which part is interference.
    Tomas Vale: A radio can make a poor voice sound convincing. It can also make a perfectly ordinary voice sound like someone calling from the bottom of a well.
<<endif>>


-> Compare the records from the quay. <<if $gh_clue_bell_log or $gh_clue_cold_catch or $gh_clue_hull_marks or $gh_clue_net_return>>
    <<if $gh_clue_bell_log and $gh_clue_cold_catch and $gh_clue_hull_marks and $gh_clue_net_return>>
        Tomas Vale: A maintenance log, temperature notes, hull measurements and a piece of net. Different tools, different witnesses. If they point in the same direction, that is worth investigating. It still isn't proof of a monster.
    <<else>>
        Tomas Vale: We have fragments, not a pattern. A log, a catch, a mark or a piece of line can tell us what happened to that one thing. It cannot explain the whole sea.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>
<<set $gh_met_tomas = true>>
<<endif>>
===

title: Greyhaven_NetMender
---
<<if $npc_relationship < 15>>
Inez Sarmiento: I have no work for you. Please leave my tools alone.
-> Leave.
<<else>>
<<if not $gh_met_inez>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Mind the mesh. I don't know you yet, so please keep your hands away from the line.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Inez Sarmiento. A torn net can be mended. A careless knot can lose the whole catch.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Mind the mesh. A torn net can be mended. A careless knot can lose the whole catch.
        Inez Sarmiento: If your lines come back cut clean, bring them to me. Don't decide what did it before you look.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I've seen your boat at the quay. Bring damaged line here before you throw it away. There may be something to learn from it.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Good to meet you properly. I can show you a few things if you're willing to look closely.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I saved a length of good line for you. You return what you borrow, and that matters here.
    <<endif>>
<<else>>
    <<if $npc_relationship < 30>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I remember you. I will speak, but I will not pretend it never happened.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Show me the line. I can tell a snag from a cut if there's enough left to examine.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Back again? Put the line down where I can see the whole length.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I kept a place for your gear. What have you brought me today?
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 90>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I was hoping you'd come by. There's a repair technique I think you'd understand now.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: There you are. I kept the good thread aside. We can work without rushing today.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>

-> Ask how she checks a damaged net.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Look at the whole length. Reef stone frays line. A propeller can cut it. Don't judge from one torn strand.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I check the floats, the joins, the knots and the places where the net rubs against the boat. The damage has a direction.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I look for where the damage begins, not only where it ends. A snag pulls differently from a cut, and a cut that starts inside the mesh deserves a second look.
    <<endif>>
    <<if $gh_inez_trust == 0>>
        <<set $gh_inez_trust = 1>>
        <<relationship "inez.asked_about_net_damage" 2>>
    <<endif>>

-> Ask why the missing crews tied three turns around the float line.
    Inez Sarmiento: One crew used to tie three turns around the float line before leaving harbour. Their gear came back with every knot still fastened.
    Inez Sarmiento: The net was empty. Not torn. Empty.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: They did it every time, even when the water was calm. It was their habit. I can still picture the hands tying it.
    <<endif>>

-> Show her the damaged line instead of throwing it away.
    Inez Sarmiento: Good. Evidence has to be handled before anyone decides what it means. I'll remember that.
    <<relationship "inez.preserved_damaged_line" 4>>

-> Dismiss her findings without looking.
    Inez Sarmiento: Then I have nothing useful to add. Carelessness does not become knowledge just because it is confident.
    <<relationship "inez.dismissed_net_evidence" -3>>

-> Ask what a normal workday looks like.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Sort the line, find the weak places, mend what can be mended. Then start again.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I sort by material and damage, then repair the parts that can still carry a proper load. The rest is cut away and replaced.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I check the nets before the market wakes, repair through the warm part of the day, then leave the tools ready for whoever needs them next.
        Inez Sarmiento: It is quiet work. I like knowing exactly what my hands have changed.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask how to tell fraying from a clean cut.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 60>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Fraying leaves fibres worn and uneven. A cut can leave a sharper edge. Bring me the piece if you want a careful answer.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Fraying often shows repeated wear across fibres. A cut may cross them more cleanly. But water, tension and old damage can confuse the picture.
        Inez Sarmiento: I won't identify a cause from one strand. I need the whole section.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask about her mother's knots. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Inez Sarmiento: My mother taught me to check a knot after I tied it, then check the line beside it. A strong knot is no use if the rope beside it is worn through.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: She used to say, "A knot is a promise made by your hands." I still hear her whenever I finish a repair.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask why she keeps damaged pieces instead of throwing them away. <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
    Inez Sarmiento: Because the damage is part of the record. If I throw it away, all I have left is someone's memory of what happened.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
        Inez Sarmiento: I keep a small bundle of the strange cuts. I don't know what made them. I only know they don't match the usual wear.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask for one of her net-mending sayings.
    <<if $npc_relationship < 45>>
        Inez Sarmiento: "Look at the line before you blame the water." That's a good place to start.
    <<elseif $npc_relationship < 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: "A net is only as strong as the knot nobody checks."
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: My mother used to say, "The sea tests every knot, but it never ties one for you." I think about that when the weather turns.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what she would teach someone who wants to earn her trust. <<if $npc_relationship >= 90>>
    Inez Sarmiento: Bring the whole damaged section, not only the torn end. Don't hide the part that makes your mistake easier to explain.
    Inez Sarmiento: Then let the evidence change your mind. That's how I know someone is ready to learn.

-> Ask about local rules.
    Inez Sarmiento: If the birds leave the water all at once, don't follow the fish. Check the wind. Check the current. Then decide whether you still want to be out there.

-> Ask what crews check before leaving harbour.
    Inez Sarmiento: Floats, joins, knots and the anchor points. A net can look sound on the rack and still fail when the boat turns.
    <<if $npc_relationship >= 75>>
        Inez Sarmiento: The careful crews checked the same places twice. They knew a small repair at the quay was cheaper than losing a catch offshore.
    <<endif>>

-> Leave.
    Inez Sarmiento: Bring the damaged line back. Don't throw away evidence just because it frightens you.

-> Ask what the missing crew's returned net tells her.
    <<if not $gh_clue_net_return>>
        Inez Sarmiento: The net came back empty, but the knots were still fastened and the mesh was not torn. That is what I can show you.
        Inez Sarmiento: Three turns on the float line was their habit. It was a practical securing knot, not a charm. I will not pretend the empty net tells me where the catch went.
        <<set $gh_clue_net_return = true>>
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: Empty, not torn, knots still set. I have not found a better description, and I won't invent one.
    <<endif>>

-> Ask what she makes when she is not mending nets. <<if $npc_relationship >= 60>>
    Inez Sarmiento: Little woven birds from short pieces of sound line. My mother taught me. They are no use for fishing, which is why I like making them.
    Inez Sarmiento: Not every useful thing has to earn its place by surviving a storm.
<<endif>>


-> Compare the records from the quay. <<if $gh_clue_bell_log or $gh_clue_cold_catch or $gh_clue_hull_marks or $gh_clue_net_return>>
    <<if $gh_clue_bell_log and $gh_clue_cold_catch and $gh_clue_hull_marks and $gh_clue_net_return>>
        Inez Sarmiento: The records should stay separate until we know more. But the bell log, the cold catch, the hull marks and the empty net deserve to be kept together—not explained away one by one.
    <<else>>
        Inez Sarmiento: We have fragments, not a pattern. A log, a catch, a mark or a piece of line can tell us what happened to that one thing. It cannot explain the whole sea.
    <<endif>>
<<endif>>
<<set $gh_met_inez = true>>
<<endif>>
===
